\"do not wait on main CI before merge\"), and the unbuffered launcher. #2139 stays blocked-open.\n\n## Delta over c0997e256\n\nCommit `4149e28eb` adds, in `tools/audit/seat/merge_train.py` and `tools/audit/seat/train.sh`:\n\n- `batch --skip-bad`: a Stop an entry raises in its own admission, recarry, carry, CI wait or merge logs `SKIP #<pr> <phase>: <reason>` and the train continues with the next entry; the run closes with `<state-dir>/skipped.json` and a `SKIP REPORT: N skipped of M` line, and rc is 1 — the queue ran to its end, but a caller that treats rc 0 as nothing-to-look-at must be told an entry was left behind (the local reference `fix/train-skip-bad` 3e0a4be54 returned 0 there; adapted per the owner's direction that rc reflects skips). Two Stops are never skippable: a red MAIN (the step-0 gate and the merge guard's re-read stay global) and a Stop AFTER the merge API call, fatal for its entry alone, logged `post-merge`, never retried.\n- `batch --overlap`: `main_green` becomes log-only, and every serial entry is re-carried UP FRONT at batch start so all their pull_request runs fire concurrently; entries then land one at a time in queue order as each head's own required contexts conclude. This encodes the two measured facts: main's post-merge required run blocks the next entry via `main_green` (log-only under the flag), and the ruleset still blocks `gh pr merge` until the PR's OWN required contexts conclude at the recarry head — no flag bypasses a ruleset, so the win is overlap. An entry recarried up front guards its merge on its recarry base remaining main's ancestor: main may advance only by this batch's own merges. An earlier queue entry still pending is never jumped by a later eligible one.\n- The launcher runs the child `python3 -u` and passes `--skip-bad`/`--overlap` through.\n- `one()` is refactored into `recarry()` + `at_head()` so `one_overlap()` shares the CI/carry/land tail instead of copying it; argparse moved into `_parser()` so the self-test pins